WEBVTT

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.137
Hi, my name is Jessie.
嗨，我叫杰西。

00:00:03.137 --> 00:00:04.103
I'm in the US.
我在美国。

00:00:04.103 --> 00:00:04.603
I'm 22.
我22岁了。

00:00:04.603 --> 00:00:08.397
I've been watching how I met your mother since I was 9.
我从九岁起就一直在关注我是如何认识你母亲的。

00:00:08.397 --> 00:00:13.363
My loved ones would probably say that I'm weird about the show and I am.
我所爱的人可能会说我对这个节目很奇怪，我确实如此。

00:00:13.363 --> 00:00:24.263
My family moved a lot when I was young and I watched the show again and again and again and again and the colors of him and the characters and the vernacular.
当我年轻的时候，我的家人搬了很多家，我一遍又一遍地看这部剧，一遍又一遍地观看他的色彩、人物和白话。

00:00:24.263 --> 00:00:31.437
The apartment and the booth all constitute a very real and very consistent location in my mind and body.
公寓和展位都在我的身心中构成了一个非常真实、非常一致的位置。

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.179
when I don't have another one of those.
当我没有其中的另一件时。

00:00:34.179 --> 00:00:48.939
I'm trans, and I'm not gonna say that Hemium was a razor show that uplifts trans people, but I will say that every time I've watched the world shift against me, I found myself coming home to this show again, and again, and again, and again.
我是跨性别者，我不会说 Hemium 是一个提升跨性别者精神的剃须刀节目，但我会说，每次我看到世界对我不利时，我发现自己一次又一次地回到这个节目。

00:00:48.939 --> 00:01:09.120
I'm alone, I'll be soon, in New York City When I see you, please permit me to tell you everything in New York City.
我独自一人，我很快就会在纽约。当我见到你时，请允许我告诉你在纽约的一切。

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:16.079
Thank you so much for that note, Jesse, truly moving to me and Craig.
非常感谢你的留言，杰西，真的让我和克雷格感动。

00:01:16.079 --> 00:01:24.680
Welcome to How We Made Your Mother, where we discuss the TV show, How I Met Your Mother, which I starred in from 2005 to 2018.
欢迎来到《我们是如何塑造你的母亲》，我们在这里讨论我在 2005 年至 2018 年主演的电视节目《老爸老妈的浪漫史》。

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.079
Oh, 2014 as Ted Mosvie.
哦，2014 年饰演 Ted Mosvie。

00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:30.079
Craig Thomas co-created the show with Carter Bays.
克雷格·托马斯与卡特·贝斯共同创作了该剧。

00:01:30.079 --> 00:01:31.519
He's my co-host, hi Craig.
他是我的共同主持人，嗨克雷格。

00:01:31.519 --> 00:01:33.040
Hey everybody, hey Joshy.
嘿大家，嘿乔希。

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.359
Hey, we're joined by Alec Lev are intrepid.
嘿，勇敢的亚历克·列夫 (Alec Lev) 加入了我们。

00:01:36.359 --> 00:01:39.159
I don't even know what that word means, but you feel intrepid to me.
我什至不知道这个词是什么意思，但你对我来说很勇敢。

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:40.319
Is that something you are?
你就是这样的人吗？

00:01:40.319 --> 00:01:41.920
And this is your star episode.
这是你的明星剧集。

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.000
This week, if any, I am intrepid.
这周，如果有的话，我是勇敢的。

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:44.840
Yes.
是的。

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:45.680
He's intrepid.
他很勇敢。

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.879
Welcome, Alec Lev.
欢迎，亚历克·列夫。

00:01:46.879 --> 00:01:49.239
Thank you so much, Jesse, for that voice note.
非常感谢你，杰西，的语音留言。

00:01:49.239 --> 00:01:50.560
We're very moved by it.
我们对此非常感动。

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.200
Craig, what do you, what's that provoking you?
克雷格，你在做什么，是什么在激怒你？

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:57.439
So much gratitude is what it is, the first thing it provokes.
如此多的感激之情就是它本身，它引起的第一件事。

00:01:57.439 --> 00:02:02.439
And then the desire to apologize a little bit.
然后想要道歉一点。

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.400
I'll start with the gratitude and get to the apology.
我将从感谢开始，然后是道歉。

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:31.936
The gratitude is that somebody like Jesse could watch the show and feel that connection to the show while also seeing certain jokes and moments in the show that were, I think, clunky and ignorant where we made jokes that were sort of flippant and I think offensive to the trans community in ways that at the time, 20 years ago, we were, we as a writing staff, Carter and I were not, not aware that we were doing.
令人感激的是，像杰西这样的人可以观看这部剧，感受到与这部剧的联系，同时也看到剧中的某些笑话和时刻，我认为这些笑话和时刻是笨拙和无知的，我们开的玩笑有点轻率，我认为这些笑话对跨性别群体的冒犯是在20年前，我们作为编剧人员，卡特和我都没有意识到我们在这样做。

00:02:31.936 --> 00:02:40.056
And it's, I think it's so important 20 years later, like part of looking back on this show is, you know, owning some of that stuff.
我认为 20 年后这一点非常重要，就像回顾这部剧的一部分，你知道，拥有其中的一些东西。

00:02:40.056 --> 00:02:48.981
The idea that we get a message from somebody like Jesse, and we hear the love of the show and also the honest reaction to some of those moments.
我们从像杰西这样的人那里得到了信息，我们听到了对这部剧的热爱，以及对其中一些时刻的诚实反应。

00:02:48.981 --> 00:02:55.428
And I've heard we've heard this before from members of the trans community, all of whom deserve respect.
我听说我们以前从跨性别群体的成员那里听到过这样的说法，他们都值得尊重。

00:02:55.428 --> 00:03:02.679
And they don't need to turn on a comedy show and see jokes that that might be using terms that are offensive to them.
他们不需要打开喜剧节目并看到可能使用冒犯他们的术语的笑话。

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:14.159
And we did that a couple of times, very ignorantly, just like to say sorry to Jesse and to anybody else in the trans community out there who felt at all hurt or other or pushed away from the show.
我们这样做了几次，非常无知，只是想向杰西和跨性别社区中任何其他感到受到伤害或其他或被推离节目的人说声抱歉。

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:20.719
It is a show, you know, one of my favorite lines from How I Met Your Mother is, love is the best thing we do.
这是一场演出，你知道，《老爸老妈的浪漫史》中我最喜欢的台词之一是，爱是我们所做的最好的事情。

00:03:20.719 --> 00:03:23.159
I think that's the beating heart of the show.
我认为这就是这部剧的核心。

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:28.840
And we want everyone to feel loved and respected and welcomed into our little world here.
我们希望每个人都能感受到爱、尊重和欢迎来到我们的小世界。

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:40.840
I'm so glad that Jesse was still welcome, still was part of it and felt inside of the show even as we made some mistakes and so I would just like to own those mistakes and say thanks.
我很高兴杰西仍然受到欢迎，仍然是其中的一部分，并且在我们犯了一些错误的情况下感受到了节目的内在，所以我只想承认这些错误并表示感谢。

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:41.840
Yeah.
是的。

00:03:41.840 --> 00:04:00.487
You know, it's interesting, it makes me reflect on if you're a member of any kind of marginalized community or minority, if you want to love cultural artifacts, there's a lot of stuff you're gonna have to kind of like skip over or jump over or swerve around, you know?
你知道，这很有趣，这让我反思，如果你是任何边缘化社区或少数群体的成员，如果你想热爱文化艺术品，有很多东西你必须跳过或跳过或转向，你知道吗？

00:04:00.487 --> 00:04:08.906
Like minority, you know, non-white people had to find themselves, even in our show, in white character stories, you know?
就像少数族裔一样，你知道，非白人必须找到自己，即使是在我们的节目中，在白人角色的故事中，你知道吗？

00:04:08.906 --> 00:04:34.650
And the beauty of the moment we're in, or maybe, I mean, they're certainly pushed back against this, which is not good at all, but that there are stories targeted more specifically to the people that they're meant for and it gives us an opportunity to watch, you know, a gay story and be like, ah, totally relate, totally moved by it, totally have felt all those things.
我们所处的时刻之美，或者也许，我的意思是，他们肯定会反对这一点，这一点都不好，但是有一些故事更具体地针对他们的目标人群，这让我们有机会观看，你知道，一个同性恋故事，然后就像，啊，完全相关，完全被它感动，完全感受到所有这些事情。

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:41.817
So I think in some ways, the more specific we can be, the more, it's the classic writing advice, right?
所以我认为在某些方面，我们越具体，就越是经典的写作建议，对吧？

00:04:41.817 --> 00:04:46.131
Like the more specific you are, the more universal you become.
就像你越具体，你就越普遍。

00:04:46.131 --> 00:04:57.960
But I think that, you You know, like, I've always been struck by the fact that it is not white 20 and 30 somethings in urban centers that only love how I met your mother.
但我认为，你知道，我一直对这样一个事实感到震惊：城市中心的 20 岁和 30 岁左右的白人并不只喜欢我遇见你母亲的方式。

00:04:57.959 --> 00:05:00.839
That is not the fan, that is not the exclusive fan base.
那不是粉丝，那不是专属的粉丝群。

00:05:00.839 --> 00:05:05.759
So it does transcend its character's demos.
所以它确实超越了角色的演示。

00:05:05.759 --> 00:05:06.600
Yeah, it does.
是的，确实如此。

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:15.360
And when we hear from people from other parts of life who still relate to the show, And it's the thing that makes me the happiest.
当我们听到生活中其他地方的人们仍然对这个节目感兴趣时，这就是让我最高兴的事情。

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:43.240
And when I realized that when there are moments that come up that I wish I could go back in a time machine and change, one of the reasons we're talking about this in addition to Jesse's wonderful message was that in the previous week's episode, which was Mary the Paralegal, there is a joke where Barney says a term that is derogatory towards the trans community that at the time, 20 years ago, I just think we didn't, we were too ignorant to know that And it felt that way.
当我意识到，当有些时刻出现时，我希望我可以乘坐时光机回到过去并改变，除了杰西精彩的信息之外，我们谈论这个的原因之一是在上周的节目中，即律师助理玛丽，有一个笑话，巴尼说了一个贬低跨性别群体的术语，当时，20年前，我只是认为我们没有，我们太无知了，不知道它，而且感觉就是这样。

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.199
The sad part is it didn't even occur to anyone to flag it.
可悲的是，甚至没有人想到要标记它。

00:05:46.199 --> 00:06:05.959
No, no, no, nobody, and nobody flagged it, nobody thought twice about it, which is such a portrait of 20 years ago is a long time, and yet also look where we are as a society right now in 2025 and all the battles that are being fought for people's identity and to have respect and to be to not be marginalized, to just be able to be human.
不，不，不，没有人，没有人提出这个问题，没有人再三考虑，这是 20 年前的写照，已经是很长一段时间了，但也要看看 2025 年我们作为一个社会现在所处的位置，以及所有为人们的身份、尊重、不被边缘化、能够成为人类而进行的战斗。

00:06:05.959 --> 00:06:14.379
And I wish I could wave a magic wand and take anything out of the show that ever made anyone feel marginalized or outside of it.
我希望我能挥舞魔杖，从节目中拿走任何让任何人感到被边缘化或置身于节目之外的东西。

00:06:14.379 --> 00:06:16.579
Do you remember, Craig and happy, thank you more, please.
你还记得吗，克雷格和快乐，请多谢你。

00:06:16.579 --> 00:06:24.639
There's a talk that my character has with Pablo Schreiber, where I say a writer told me once that every five years you realize what an asshole you were five years ago.
我的角色和巴勃罗·施赖伯有过一次谈话，我说一位作家曾经告诉我，每五年你就会意识到五年前你是一个多么混蛋。

00:06:24.639 --> 00:06:30.860
And it's just this continuing process of like, what an asshole I was five years ago.
这就是一个持续的过程，就像五年前我是多么混蛋一样。

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:32.060
Four sets of those five years ago.
五年前的四套。

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:33.060
Yeah, exactly.
是的，完全正确。

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:39.620
So I think about like the jokes we were making my freshman year of college in that freshman dorm.
所以我想起了大学一年级时我们在新生宿舍里开的玩笑。

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:46.500
And like, there's not maybe one guy who I can think of would still be making those jokes, but no one else would be.
就像，我能想到的也许没有一个人还会开这些玩笑，但其他人也不会。

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:50.459
And I think like, it's a good thing that comedy changes.
我认为，喜剧的改变是一件好事。

00:06:50.459 --> 00:06:55.339
Like, I don't throw in with those comedians who are like, you can't say anything anymore.
就像，我不会加入那些像“你不能再说什么了”的喜剧演员。

00:06:55.339 --> 00:06:57.819
It's like, no, you just gotta get better.
就像，不，你必须变得更好。

00:06:57.819 --> 00:06:58.660
You just gotta get better.
你必须变得更好。

00:06:58.660 --> 00:06:59.500
I totally agree.
我完全同意。

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:09.980
So between year two and three of how much mother my son was born and he has an intellectual disability And health, multiple disabilities, health wise too.
因此，在我儿子出生的第二年和第三年之间，他有智力障碍和健康，多重残疾，健康方面也是如此。

00:07:09.980 --> 00:07:45.110
And the way that changed my world, the paradigm shift that that was for me, you know, this kind of privileged white guy grew up among islands, you know, Irish American, like I have fairly sheltered, college kind of opened my world so much more, but I think having Elliot, having my son is the thing that really kicked the door open to realizing what it feels like to be on the receiving end of dehumanization in terms that are derogatory towards people with disabilities, to have the, there's that great line that's like, having a child is to have your heart forever walk around outside your body.
改变我的世界的方式，对我来说的范式转变，你知道，这种享有特权的白人在岛屿中长大，你知道，爱尔兰裔美国人，就像我得到了相当的庇护一样，大学让我的世界更加开放，但我认为有埃利奥特，有我的儿子是真正打开大门的事情，让我意识到成为贬低残疾人的非人化接收端的感觉，有那条伟大的路线，就像，生孩子就是让你的心永远在你的身体之外走来走去。

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:47.350
And that is what parenthood feels like.
这就是为人父母的感觉。

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:56.629
And if that child happens to be a little more vulnerable, a little more fragile, it's just magnified, but I think all parenthood really feels that way.
如果那个孩子碰巧更脆弱一点，更脆弱一点，它就会被放大，但我认为所有为人父母的人都会有这种感觉。

00:07:56.629 --> 00:08:11.698
And like, the way that people with disabilities are spoken about and joked about marginalized and dehumanized in some of our pop culture vernacular and some of our political discourse now too is horrifying.
就像，在我们的一些流行文化方言和我们现在的一些政治话语中，谈论和开玩笑残疾人被边缘化和非人化的方式也令人恐惧。

00:08:11.698 --> 00:08:21.865
And it's something that I didn't realize how horrifying it was until I was in that world until my heart was walking around outside my body.
直到我身处那个世界，直到我的心在我的身体之外走来走去，我才意识到这是多么可怕。

00:08:21.865 --> 00:08:26.400
And that's what I've had 18 years of that now of that feeling.
18年来我一直有这种感觉。

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.569
And yeah, sorry, I'm getting choked up thinking about it.
是的，抱歉，一想到这个我就哽咽了。

00:08:30.569 --> 00:08:32.730
It just, it changes everything, right?
它只是，它改变了一切，对吧？

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.090
It changes everything to be in that.
它改变了一切。

00:08:35.090 --> 00:08:44.889
And not just about the, you know, in a lot of ways, like marginalized people stand with other marginalized people because they know, right?
不仅仅是，你知道，在很多方面，边缘化的人与其他边缘化的人站在一起，因为他们知道，对吗？

00:08:44.889 --> 00:08:46.250
They have the experience.
他们有经验。

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:50.250
So it didn't just give you the experience of like, oh, I don't want anyone to say these particular words.
所以它不仅仅给你这样的体验，哦，我不想让任何人说这些特殊的话。

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:53.570
It was like, no, let's not, let's get rid of dehumanizing language.
就像，不，我们不要，让我们摆脱非人化的语言。

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:57.250
Like, let's reconsider the whole lexicon, right?
就像，让我们重新考虑整个词典，对吧？

00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:19.360
Yeah, you can't ask, yeah, it has to be across every, it has to be all can all equal parts across every sort of marginalized type of person, like when you have, yeah, I just think it's my glimpse into how it feels to be marginalized or to be on the receiving end of language that is sort of dehumanizing or devaluing.
是的，你不能问，是的，它必须遍及每一个人，它必须是所有边缘化类型的人的所有平等部分，就像当你有，是的，我只是认为这是我对被边缘化或处于某种非人性或贬低语言的接收端的感觉的一瞥。

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:22.039
And I hate that there's some moments like that on the show.
我讨厌剧中出现这样的时刻。

00:09:22.039 --> 00:09:25.919
It certainly wasn't the intention, but I think it's really important to own those moments.
这当然不是故意的，但我认为拥有这些时刻非常重要。

00:09:25.919 --> 00:09:27.519
And I think you don't know till you know.
我认为在你知道之前你不会知道。

00:09:27.519 --> 00:09:32.200
I think you, and once that door is open, you kind of see things much more clearly.
我想你，一旦那扇门打开，你就会更清楚地看到事情。

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:37.240
And so whenever we hear about this, I'm always, my instinct is to immediately say, yeah, I get it.
所以每当我们听到这个消息时，我总是本能地立即说，是的，我明白了。

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:40.679
I'm sorry because I think we've evolved and we've come a long way.
我很抱歉，因为我认为我们已经进化并且已经取得了长足的进步。

00:09:40.679 --> 00:09:44.600
I know Carter Bay is my co-creator of the show and one of my best friends.
我知道卡特贝是我的节目的共同创作者，也是我最好的朋友之一。

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:46.159
I know this matters to him so much.
我知道这对他来说非常重要。

00:09:46.159 --> 00:09:51.440
I know he feels this way and he would be echoing these words and feel the same way towards Jesse's message.
我知道他有这种感觉，他也会重复这些话，并对杰西的信息有同样的感觉。

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:53.600
And so I just want to include him in.
所以我只想把他也包括在内。

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:54.600
us.
我们。

00:09:54.600 --> 00:10:04.899
And I wrote you this little day to sing to you in New York City.
今天我写信给你，是为了在纽约为你唱歌。

00:10:04.899 --> 00:10:13.779
We'll be right back.
我们马上回来。

00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:16.360
And now back to the show.
现在回到节目。

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:18.759
Craig, you wrote such a beautiful piece.
克雷格，你写了一篇如此美丽的文章。

00:10:18.759 --> 00:10:19.840
Was it in the LA Times?
是在《洛杉矶时报》上吗？

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:20.840
Where did that piece run?
那块跑到哪里去了？

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:22.340
Oh, the Boston Globe.
哦，波士顿环球报。

00:10:22.340 --> 00:10:23.340
Yeah.
是的。

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:24.340
Oh, the Boston Globe.
哦，波士顿环球报。

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:25.340
Yeah.
是的。

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:27.240
If you can seek this out, maybe Alec, we can put this in the show notes.
如果你能找到这个，也许亚历克，我们可以把它放在演出笔记中。

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:28.240
Craig's article.
克雷格的文章。

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:29.240
Yeah.
是的。

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:30.919
It was called Hollywood and the R word.
它被称为好莱坞和 R 字。

00:10:30.919 --> 00:10:35.500
And it is about a derogatory term for people with intellectual disabilities.
这是对智障人士的贬义词。

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:36.500
And this is what I mean.
这就是我的意思。

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:37.860
I don't like the comedy defense of it either.
我也不喜欢它的喜剧辩护。

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.100
We can't make jokes anymore.
我们不能再开玩笑了。

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:40.860
It's just a funny word.
这只是一个有趣的词。

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:45.500
And certainly that's what it, I'm sure that's how we felt in the writer's room 20 years ago.
当然就是这样，我确信这就是 20 年前我们在作家房间里的感受。

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:46.500
And we're like, ah, it's just a funny word to put it.
我们就像，啊，这只是一个有趣的词。

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:47.500
And no, it's not.
不，不是。

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:48.500
It's not a funny word.
这不是一个有趣的词。

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:49.940
There's no defense of it.
对此没有任何辩护。

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:53.580
Once it's a joke that can hurt someone, find another joke.
一旦一个笑话会伤害别人，就找另一个笑话。

00:10:53.580 --> 00:11:02.330
If you realize you're making a joke that hurt someone and like, I think there's this other defense of this where people would say, that's what that character would say, right?
如果你意识到你正在开一个伤害某人的玩笑，我想还有另一种辩护，人们会说，这就是那个角色会说的，对吗？

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:06.629
Or that fits the story or that fits the theme so the character gets to talk like that.
或者适合故事或主题，这样角色就可以这样说话。

00:11:06.629 --> 00:11:12.179
I'm not going to defend our use of a word like that because there's nothing important to that saying that word.
我不会为我们使用这样的词辩护，因为说这个词没有什么重要的。

00:11:12.179 --> 00:11:14.339
It's just a dumb, meaningless joke.
这只是一个愚蠢的、毫无意义的笑话。

00:11:14.339 --> 00:11:15.696
It's a throwaway joke.
这是一个一次性的笑话。

00:11:15.696 --> 00:11:17.299
It could be anything else.
也可以是其他任何东西。

00:11:17.299 --> 00:11:28.179
If you take the 10,000 jokes Barney made in 208 episode and you take that one joke away, It's not like suddenly no one can understand Barney.
如果你把巴尼在第 208 集中讲的 10,000 个笑话拿走，然后把其中一个笑话拿走，这并不是说突然之间就没有人能理解巴尼了。

00:11:28.179 --> 00:11:32.740
Like he's not even sharp enough for me without that joke, right?
如果没有那个笑话，他对我来说就不够敏锐，对吧？

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:34.220
It is a very clear character.
这是一个非常明确的角色。

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:35.259
He doesn't need to make that joke.
他没必要开那样的玩笑。

00:11:35.259 --> 00:11:38.299
And so therefore that joke has no nutritional value.
因此这个笑话没有营养价值。

00:11:38.299 --> 00:11:39.899
That joke isn't satire.
这个笑话不是讽刺。

00:11:39.899 --> 00:11:41.620
It's not particularly character defining.
它不是特别定义角色。

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:47.259
It's not because of the episodes about that or that's how this group of people would talk.
这并不是因为有关此事的情节，也不是因为这群人会这样说话。

00:11:47.259 --> 00:11:49.139
I don't think it serves any purpose.
我不认为它有任何目的。

00:11:49.139 --> 00:11:53.940
And I think when comedy people try to defend, let me, we have to be able to say every word.
我认为当喜剧人们试图捍卫时，让我来说，我们必须能够说出每一个字。

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:59.220
I think there should be intention and meaning behind the use of any term that could potentially offend somebody.
我认为使用任何可能冒犯某人的术语背后都应该有意图和意义。

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:00.620
I think there's art.
我认为有艺术。

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:11.379
You look back at Huckleberry Finn and Mark Twain, he was writing a certain way to capture a certain moment, a certain vernacular, judging it by modern day standards and not understanding the satire behind it.
你回顾一下哈克贝利·费恩和马克·吐温，他以某种方式写作来捕捉某个时刻、某种方言，用现代标准来评判它，而不理解其背后的讽刺。

00:12:11.379 --> 00:12:13.220
That's something completely different.
那是完全不同的事情。

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:19.740
But I just think if there's no real value to it and you're just throwing it out there, it's the empty shock value.
但我只是认为，如果它没有真正的价值，而你只是把它扔在那里，那就是空洞的冲击价值。

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:23.259
That wasn't your encounter's most tween-like moment.
那不是你们相遇中最像青少年的时刻。

00:12:23.259 --> 00:12:24.285
No, no, it's not.
不，不，不是。

00:12:24.285 --> 00:12:25.250
It's not satire.
这不是讽刺。

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:26.577
It's not for a reason.
这不是有原因的。

00:12:26.577 --> 00:12:35.442
If it's not for a reason and you can't explain the reason it's there in an artful way, you certainly shouldn't be using it and maybe not even then.
如果它不是有原因的，并且您无法以巧妙的方式解释它存在的原因，那么您当然不应该使用它，甚至可能也不应该使用它。

00:12:35.442 --> 00:12:37.854
But yeah, it's just one of those things.
但是，是的，这只是其中之一。

00:12:37.854 --> 00:12:40.870
I think you have to own it and you have to evolve.
我认为你必须拥有它并且你必须不断发展。

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:46.901
There's a British comedian named David Bideal who wrote a really great book called Jews Don't Count.
有一位名叫大卫·比德尔的英国喜剧演员写了一本非常伟大的书，名叫《犹太人不重要》。

00:12:46.901 --> 00:12:51.786
And he's just been writing and speaking a lot about anti-Semitism over the years.
多年来他一直在撰写和谈论大量有关反犹太主义的文章。

00:12:51.786 --> 00:12:57.938
And I saw this clip of something where he said, there's two jokes I used to tell, or that I have told.
我看到了这段视频，他说，有两个笑话我曾经讲过，或者我曾经讲过。

00:12:57.938 --> 00:13:02.220
The first joke relies upon the kind of stereotype about Jews and money.
第一个笑话依赖于对犹太人和金钱的刻板印象。

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:13.280
The second joke, which is actually quite funny, which I'll tell, which is a German, a French person and a Jewish guy are sitting on a bench, and it's a sweltering hot day.
第二个笑话，实际上很有趣，我会讲，这是一个德国人，一个法国人和一个犹太人坐在长凳上，这是一个闷热的大热天。

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:16.061
And the Frenchman says, he says, I'm tired.
法国人说，他说，我累了。

00:13:16.061 --> 00:13:16.837
I'm thirsty.
我渴了。

00:13:16.837 --> 00:13:17.937
I must have wine.
我必须有酒。

00:13:17.937 --> 00:13:19.683
The German says, I'm tired.
德国人说，我累了。

00:13:19.683 --> 00:13:20.459
I'm thirsty.
我渴了。

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:21.383
I must have beer.
我必须喝啤酒。

00:13:21.383 --> 00:13:23.067
The Jewish guy says, I'm tired.
犹太人说，我累了。

00:13:23.067 --> 00:13:23.719
I'm thirsty.
我渴了。

00:13:23.719 --> 00:13:24.859
I must have diabetes.
我一定有糖尿病。

00:13:27.419 --> 00:13:36.346
The reason he says, I'm going to keep, I will still tell that joke is because no one's killed Jews over their hypochondriacal tendencies.
他之所以说，我会继续讲这个笑话，是因为没有人因为犹太人的疑病症倾向而杀害他们。

00:13:36.346 --> 00:13:36.846
Right.
正确的。

00:13:36.846 --> 00:13:46.945
But there have been, there's a long trail of blood over this idea that Jews control things and have all the money and all that, that pernicious stereotype.
但犹太人控制一切、拥有所有金钱等观念，这种有害的刻板印象一直以来都存在着血腥的痕迹。

00:13:46.945 --> 00:14:08.187
So it almost is like, you know, if you go into a marginalized community, they're joking about themselves, right, in ways that they are allowed to, that they are, you know, it's so different when, you know, when someone tells a Jewish joke, I'm always like, uh oh, you know, if they're not Jewish, even sometimes when they are.
所以这几乎就像，你知道，如果你进入一个边缘化社区，他们会以他们被允许的方式开玩笑，他们是，你知道，当有人讲犹太笑话时，情况是如此不同，我总是会说，哦哦，你知道，如果他们不是犹太人，即使有时他们是犹太人。

00:14:08.187 --> 00:14:16.071
But yeah, I think that there are stereotypes that are for the, they're fun and they're like, we can enjoy them, you know?
但是，是的，我认为存在一些刻板印象，它们很有趣，我们可以享受它们，你知道吗？

00:14:16.071 --> 00:14:19.394
And there's other ones that people get killed over.
还有其他一些事情会导致人们被杀。

00:14:19.394 --> 00:14:23.369
And we have to be clear about where we're telling jokes from.
我们必须清楚我们讲笑话的来源。

00:14:23.259 --> 00:14:24.259
Yes.
是的。

00:14:24.259 --> 00:14:33.825
I think also what Craig said about intention that if the marginalized communities is making a joke at their own expense, it is a bonding exercise.
我也认为克雷格所说的意图是，如果边缘化社区以自己的利益开个玩笑，那么这是一种联系活动。

00:14:33.825 --> 00:14:39.460
That's not what it is when someone from outside the community is tearing someone down.
当社区外的人拆毁某人时，情况并非如此。

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:44.980
I get into these tussles about the R word because, yeah, I'm 18 years into being the parent of a child with a disability.
我之所以会为“R”这个词而争论不休，是因为，是的，我已经 18 岁了，已经成为一名残疾儿童的父母。

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:53.980
And so I wrote that piece in the Boston Globe, for example, I have a novel coming out in November that's in part about this.
所以我在《波士顿环球报》上写了那篇文章，例如，我在 11 月出版了一本小说，部分内容就是关于这个的。

00:14:53.980 --> 00:15:00.220
And people online, there are people who yell at me and say, like, no one cares about the R word anymore.
网上的人，有人对我大喊大叫，说，好像没有人再关心 R 这个词了。

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:01.220
It's been destigmatized.
它已经被去污名化了。

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:02.220
It doesn't hurt anybody.
它不会伤害任何人。

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:03.460
We should just be able to say it.
我们应该只能说出来。

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:11.160
And of course, I know firsthand from talking to many people with Down syndrome, for talking my son who has Jacobson syndrome, they don't want to be called that word.
当然，通过与许多患有唐氏综合症的人交谈，我第一手了解到，在与我患有雅各布森综合症的儿子交谈时，他们不想被称为这个词。

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:12.840
No one wants to be called that word.
没有人愿意被称为这个词。

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:25.580
It's not okay because you're using the word to call someone else stupid or that they have no value, you know, because you're essentially using a word that sort of originated in the disability community and now you're putting it as a totally negative word on something else.
这是不好的，因为你用这个词来称呼别人愚蠢或者他们没有价值，你知道，因为你本质上使用了一个起源于残疾人社区的词，而现在你把它作为一个完全负面的词来形容其他事物。

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:26.945
That doesn't make it okay.
但这并不能解决问题。

00:15:26.945 --> 00:15:30.779
Like, I know for a fact that it hurts people in the disability community.
就像，我知道一个事实，它伤害了残疾人社区的人们。

00:15:30.779 --> 00:15:33.181
So when people come at me and say like, it's no big deal.
因此，当人们对我说类似的话时，这没什么大不了的。

00:15:33.181 --> 00:15:34.529
It's like, no, it is a big deal.
就像，不，这是一件大事。

00:15:34.529 --> 00:15:38.700
I I know for absolute fact that it hurts people in that community and they don't want to hear that.
我知道这确实伤害了那个社区的人们，他们不想听到这个。

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:44.379
It also goes to the fact that the people in the community get to set the terms, right?
这还取决于社区中的人们可以制定条款，对吗？

00:15:44.379 --> 00:15:44.860
They sure do.
他们确实这么做了。

00:15:44.860 --> 00:16:21.118
They get to set the terms of the terms and if people in that community say that word is hurtful to us in the same way that there is a thing, sorry to make this about me, but the marginalized community of which I am a part is my religion is Judaism that I was born into and you know it gets enraging when we say that's anti-Semitic and people say no it's not you know non-Jews say no it's not and it's like why are we not allowed to set the terms of those terms you know this should be up to us.
他们可以制定条款，如果那个社区的人说这个词对我们有害，就像有件事一样，很抱歉让我这样，但我所属的边缘化社区是我出生的宗教是犹太教，你知道，当我们说这是反犹太主义时，人们会说不，这不是你知道的，非犹太人说不，这就像为什么我们不允许设定条款一样您知道哪些条款，这应该由我们决定。

00:16:21.118 --> 00:16:33.889
Yeah right so bring it back to Jesse if Jesse wants to our show and felt those moments to be painful and was, and was thinking, why, why, why does that joke have to be there?
是的，所以如果杰西想要参加我们的节目并感到那些时刻很痛苦并且过去和现在都在想，为什么，为什么，为什么那个笑话必须在那里，请把它带回给杰西？

00:16:33.889 --> 00:16:34.990
I, I love that.
我，我喜欢那个。

00:16:34.990 --> 00:16:43.032
That's how I feel when it's something I watch something and I catch the R word and there's no reason for it and it's just there for empty shock value.
这就是我在看某事时的感受，当我看到“R”这个词时，没有任何理由，它只是为了空洞的震撼价值而存在。

00:16:43.032 --> 00:16:49.465
Also, those types of jokes where you're just name checking some shock value word, it's they're never the funniest jokes.
此外，那些类型的笑话，你只是名字检查一些令人震惊的价值词，它们从来都不是最有趣的笑话。

00:16:49.465 --> 00:16:51.771
In fact, they're really never funny at all.
事实上，他们真的一点都不好笑。

00:16:51.771 --> 00:17:06.836
And I think there's this weird defense sort of like in the sort of manist fear, like, like, like podcasty world of like, and a lot of stand up comedy now where it's like, there's this illusion that those are funny, that that makes it a joke, to use a shocking word makes it a joke.
我认为这种奇怪的防御有点像男性主义的恐惧，就像播客世界一样，现在有很多单口喜剧，有一种错觉，认为那些很有趣，这使它成为一个笑话，使用一个令人震惊的词使它成为一个笑话。

00:17:06.836 --> 00:17:07.908
And it just doesn't.
但事实并非如此。

00:17:07.908 --> 00:17:12.841
It's not clever, it's not surprising, there's no art, there's not an artful way of using it.
它不聪明，也不奇怪，没有艺术，没有巧妙的使用方式。

00:17:12.841 --> 00:17:53.480
And if Jesse watched our show and felt those moments to be othering and painful, and yet, saw the beauty and the comedy and the heart in the show and stuck with the show, I would argue that Jesse has given us the greatest gift we could ask for, which is looking past some mistakes and seeing kind of the beating heart that beneath those mistakes, accepting our flaws and saying, this is still my show, this is still my place, and I just want to thank Jesse for finding that and fighting through that because I've had moments where I've had to sort of, and again, we keep on using our own ways into this, but where I've had to sort of look past certain things that feel ableist or wrong to me and still realize I love something about this piece of art anyway.
如果杰西观看了我们的节目，感受到那些时刻是另类和痛苦的，然而，看到了节目中的美丽、喜剧和内心，并坚持了节目，我会说杰西给了我们我们所能要求的最好的礼物，那就是回顾一些错误，看到这些错误背后跳动的心，接受我们的缺陷，并说，这仍然是我的节目，这仍然是我的地方，我只是想感谢杰西找到了这一点并与之斗争，因为我有过这样的时刻我不得不再次，我们继续用自己的方式来做这件事，但我不得不审视某些对我来说感觉能力主义或错误的事情，并且仍然意识到我无论如何都喜欢这件艺术品。

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:59.110
And I'm going to find, I'm going to remember that I I love something about this and try to get past that.
我会发现，我会记住我喜欢这个，并尝试克服它。

00:17:59.109 --> 00:18:01.190
And I know it's not easy always.
我知道这并不总是那么容易。

00:18:01.190 --> 00:18:06.269
So I thank you for doing that and anybody else who's stuck with us through those mistakes.
所以我感谢你们这样做，也感谢所有在这些错误中与我们站在一起的人。

00:18:06.269 --> 00:18:09.109
It's a gift, it's grace that you're giving us.
这是一份礼物，是你给我们的恩典。

00:18:09.109 --> 00:18:11.390
Well, that was beautiful, Craig.
嗯，那太漂亮了，克雷格。

00:18:11.390 --> 00:18:13.470
Thank you for sharing all that.
感谢您分享这一切。

00:18:13.470 --> 00:18:15.190
And thank you again to Jesse.
再次感谢杰西。

00:18:15.190 --> 00:18:17.349
Jesse's message was a beautiful gift.
杰西的信息是一份美丽的礼物。

00:18:17.349 --> 00:18:18.190
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:18:18.190 --> 00:18:19.029
Yeah.
是的。

